  <!-- 3. DELTA NARRATIVE -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24&#8211;48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>US Central Command confirmed on September 15 that the <em>El Gaia</em> &#8212; a Panama-flagged tanker with an Indian crew, already disabled after a prior IRGC missile strike &#8212; was struck by an IRGC drone on September 12&#8211;13 while at anchorage off the Omani coast (CENTCOM press statement, Sep 15; Republic World, Sep 15). CENTCOM issued a formal statement rather than ordering a kinetic response; the Indian government filed a diplomatic protest citing casualties among Indian nationals. The Lloyd's market War Risk Joint Committee maintained its Hormuz advisory at maximum rating; no Western-allied P&amp;I insurer reinstated cover following the incident. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Iran's September 9 shipping campaign was the largest single-day maritime action of the war: PGSA &#8212; the IRGC's Persian Gulf Shipping Authority toll-enforcement arm &#8212; targeted approximately 10 vessels near Hormuz, killing one seafarer aboard the <em>Hercules Star</em> and leaving a second missing (Al Jazeera, Sep 9; Windward AIS data, Sep 9). Brent crude briefly crossed $100 per barrel on the news. Windward AIS recorded approximately 7 commercial transits at Hormuz on September 9; the 10-day rolling average stood at approximately 13 vessels per day, well below the 30/day resolution threshold, with no single day in the September 3&#8211;16 period approaching that level and 17 days remaining before the October 3 horizon. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>Iranian Foreign Minister Araghchi arrived in Beijing on September 16 for his fifth visit to China since 2024, meeting Foreign Minister Wang Yi in the first high-level Iran-China contact since the BRICS sideline exchange on September 11&#8211;13, ahead of President Xi Jinping's September 24 Washington summit with President Trump (ANI News, Sep 15; Tribune India, Sep 15). Wang Yi described the talks as "coordination of positions"; no TIER1 source reported a Chinese offer to mediate Iran-US talks directly, though the opening of the China channel is a structural development not present when F8 was created on September 15. Araghchi maintained the MOU-precondition posture publicly articulated on September 13 and has not issued a non-conditional statement on resuming talks. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>The 14-day Iran-US indirect-session window (F3, Sep 3&#8211;17) closed without a qualifying event: no TIER1 source or named mediator (Oman, Qatar, Pakistan, Switzerland, or China) reported a convened session between named US and Iranian senior officials at any point in the September 3&#8211;16 period. Message relay through Oman and Qatar continued but fell below the held-session threshold per F3 resolution criteria and ambiguity rule. Araghchi's presence in Beijing on September 16 for Wang Yi coordination talks &#8212; rather than at any Iran-US mediator venue &#8212; confirmed the absence of session infrastructure in the final hours of the window. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Times of Israel published a poll on September 15 showing Likud and Yashar tied at 23 seats each &#8212; the first 23-23 result recorded in any cross-pollster survey of this campaign cycle since candidate-list closure (ToI, Sep 15). Channel 12 (Sep 8) and Haaretz (Sep 8) showed Yashar at 24 seats with Likud at 22; an i24NEWS/Direct Polls survey (Sep 9) recorded Likud 27, Yashar 22, an outlier not reproduced in any concurrent poll. The updated poll-of-polls average narrows slightly to Yashar approximately 23.5, Likud approximately 22.5, from Yashar approximately 23, Likud approximately 21&#8211;22 in the September 3&#8211;8 period; the ToI tie and i24NEWS outlier together constitute the first cross-pollster evidence of marginal Likud improvement. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 4. SCENARIO MAP -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:28%;">Escalation 28%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:62%;">Protracted 62%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:10%;">De-esc 10%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (28%):</strong> F10 at 40% drives near-term escalation probability; if CENTCOM strikes IRGC targets in the Sep 16&#8211;19 window, the IRGC's established pattern of proportional maritime retaliation would likely extend the kinetic cycle through the Xi-Trump summit period.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (62%):</strong> Active kinetic exchange without diplomatic off-ramp &#8212; F8 at 20% and F9 at 13% &#8212; locks both sides into Hormuz enforcement and US airstrike cycles that are destructive but below the threshold for decisive resolution in either direction.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (10%):</strong> The China channel (Araghchi-Beijing, Xi-Trump Sep 24) is the only currently active structural pathway toward a session; F8 at 20% is the leading indicator, and its conditional probability of generating a session before Oct 15 caps near-term de-escalation probability.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5. INSIDE IRAN -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Press TV and Kayhan framed the El Gaia incident as IRGC enforcement of the PGSA toll regime, describing the renewed drone strike on the disabled tanker as a "legitimate response to repeated violations of the PGSA maritime border" (Kayhan editorial, Sep 13; Press TV dispatch, Sep 14). Tasnim carried the PGSA's assertion that the <em>El Gaia</em>'s crew had "knowingly entered a restricted zone" despite anchor status off the Omani coast. Coverage was consistent across IRNA, Press TV, Tasnim, and Fars; no outlet acknowledged the CENTCOM September 15 press statement or the Indian government's diplomatic protest, and the IRGC's framing held without internal dissent in state media through September 16. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>IRNA ran full dispatches on Araghchi's September 16 Beijing arrival, framing the Wang Yi meeting as evidence that "Iranian diplomacy has not ceased despite US aggression" (IRNA, Sep 16). The state-media framing cast China as a natural advocate for Iran's position in international forums rather than a potential mediator &#8212; a distinction that could limit Beijing's ability to present itself as a neutral facilitator in any Xi-Trump summit conversation on the conflict. No IRNA dispatch from September 16 mentioned the September 24 Xi-Trump summit explicitly; the parallel framing in Tasnim and Press TV also omitted direct reference to the summit timeline. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>Supreme Leader Khamenei issued no public statement on the September 9 attack wave through September 16 &#8212; a pattern consistent with the regime's established practice of allowing IRGC maritime operations to proceed without direct Supreme Leader attribution during kinetically active phases. Ayatollah Araki, in an IRIB Friday sermon on September 12, described the Hormuz situation as "a test of Muslim nations' solidarity against American imperialism" without addressing specific IRGC actions &#8212; a framing typical of the clerical establishment's public-support posture. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

